{
  "machine_name": "FPV Racing Quadcopter",
  "assembly_instructions": "1) Lay the 0.95 m scaled lower carbon sandwich plate at y=0.155 and bolt the four 5 mm scaled X arms to it using the eight arm clamp screws. 2) Add the four aluminum frame standoffs at the plate corners, then fasten the split left and right top rails so the center stack remains open. 3) Install the four 30.5 mm-pattern stack standoffs on the bottom plate, place the 4-in-1 ESC board first, add the flight controller board above it, then add the VTX board on the upper spacers. 4) Solder or plug the four motor phase wire bundles from the ESC board to each arm and motor stator. 5) Bolt each 2207 stator to its arm tip with four screws, drop the rotor bell over the stator, then install the prop hub, three pitched blades, and prop nut on each motor. 6) Assemble the FPV camera between the two camera side cheeks, tilt it about 30 degrees upward, and connect its signal lead to the flight controller. 7) Mount the rear TPU antenna block, route the VTX coax from the VTX board, and seat the angled 5.8 GHz antenna whip clear of the prop discs. 8) In flight configuration, lower the exploded LiPo pack and strap onto the split top rails, connect the XT60 lead to the ESC input, and tighten the strap across the pack.",
  "metadata": {
    "domain": "aerial-robotics",
    "thumbnail_camera": [
      3.5,
      2.5,
      3.5
    ],
    "info": "A 5-inch-class freestyle/racing FPV quadcopter on a carbon-fiber true-X frame, scaled approximately 14x from real dimensions for legibility.",
    "facts": [
      "Wheelbase ~220 mm real, shown scaled to 2.20 m motor-to-motor across the diagonal.",
      "5 inch propellers are 127 mm diameter real, shown as 1.27 m swept discs.",
      "2207 brushless motors have 22 mm stator diameter and 7 mm stator height in real hardware.",
      "The flight stack uses a 30.5 x 30.5 mm real M3 mounting pattern, shown as a 0.305 m square scaled pattern.",
      "Carbon arms are about 5 mm thick real, shown as 0.06 m thick members for visibility.",
      "Main carbon plates are about 2 mm thick real, shown as 0.04 to 0.05 m thick scaled plates.",
      "Typical 6S 1300 mAh LiPo packs are about 75 x 35 x 35 mm real and 22.2 V nominal.",
      "The FPV camera is canted about 30 degrees upward for high-speed forward flight.",
      "Analog VTX antennas operate at 5.8 GHz and are placed aft to clear carbon and prop wash.",
      "Ready-to-fly all-up mass is commonly 550 to 650 g for this 5-inch class."
    ],
    "sources": [
      "iFlight Nazgul5 V3 5-inch quadcopter specifications",
      "T-Motor F40 PRO II / 2207 brushless motor datasheet",
      "Betaflight 4.x setup documentation for 30.5 mm flight-controller stacks",
      "Joshua Bardwell, FPV Drone Build Guide, 2023"
    ]
  },
